## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to design a scalable drift-resilient Terraform workflow across multiple AWS accounts while ensuring safe automation and auditable infrastructure changes.

## Key Concepts
- Drift detection, automatic remediation, policy enforcement (Sentinel/OPA)
- Environment-specific remote backends, provider aliasing, central baseline state
- Module architecture boundaries, testing frameworks (Kitchen-Terraform/Terratest), CI/CD gates

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "dev"
  region = "us-west-2"
}
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This assesses practical multi-cloud Terraform coordination, provider aliasing, per-cloud backends, and gating across tenants.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-cloud provider configuration with aliasing
- Per-cloud backends and remote state isolation
- CI gates that validate plans across clouds
- Enforcing region constraints and mandatory tagging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton Terraform-like snippet demonstrating provider aliases and cloud map
variable "cloud" { type = string; description = "aws or gcp" }

provider "aws" {
  region = lookup(var.cloud_map, var.cloud, {})["aws_region"]
}
provider "google" {
  region = lookup(var.cloud_map, var.cloud, {})["gcp_region"]
}

module "tenant_network" {
  source = "./modules/network"
  cloud  = var.cloud
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale adding additional clouds?
- How would you verify tagging and cost-center propagation across tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Designed to test practical skills in modular design, dynamic config-driven provisioning, and CI governance for multi-environment Terraform.

## Key Concepts
- wrapper module tag injection
- dynamic resources via for_each and config.json
- per-env backend keys
- CI gate enforcing prod constraints

## Code Example
```terraform
# wrapper module interface
variable "environment" { type = string }
variable "cost_center" { type = string }
output "injected_tags" {
  value = merge({ Environment = var.environment }, { CostCenter = var.cost_center })
}
```

```json
// config.json
{
  "dev": { "buckets": ["dev-app-data"] },
  "prod": { "buckets": ["prod-app-data"] }
}
```

```hcl
# backend config sketch
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tf-state"
    key    = "env/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate config.json before plan?
- How would you extend to include new environments without touching code?
